      correctIndex: 0,
      explanation: `<div style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; overflow:hidden"><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:8px 8px 0 0; color:white; padding:20px"><h1 style="color:white; margin:0">Finance Lease Accounting: Liability Reduction</h1></div><div style="background-color:white; padding:20px"><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Correct Answer</h3><p style="margin:0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> <span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">Minimum lease payment less the portion of the minimum lease payment allocable to interest.</span></p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f7fa; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Explanation</h3><p style="margin:0">In a finance lease, the liability is similar to a loan. Each lease payment has two components:</p><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Interest component: Based on the outstanding lease liability</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Principal component: Reduces the lease liability balance</li></ul></div><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">How Lease Liability is Reduced</h3><p style="margin:0">When a lessee makes a lease payment, the payment is applied to both interest expense and reduction of the lease liability. Only the principal portion of the payment reduces the lease liability.</p></div><div style="background-color:white; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Analysis of Answer Options</h3><table class="mx-table" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><thead><tr><th class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px">Option</th><th class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px">Analysis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Minimum lease payment plus the amortization of the related asset</td><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Incorrect. The amortization of the leased asset is a separate expense and does not affect the liability.</td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Minimum lease payment less the amortization of the related asset</td><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Incorrect. Asset amortization and liability reduction are separate processes.</td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Minimum lease payment</td><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Incorrect. This would overstate the liability reduction by including the interest portion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:0 0 8px 8px; color:white; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:white; margin:0 0 12px 0">Summary</h3><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">For a finance lease</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The lease liability is reduced by the principal portion of each lease payment</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The principal portion equals the total payment minus the interest portion</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The interest portion is calculated based on the outstanding lease liability</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Asset amortization is separate from liability reduction</li></ul><p style="margin:0">This accounting treatment parallels how loan payments reduce loan balances.</p></div></div></div>`,
